# Documentation Standards

## Documentation Structure

- **User Guides**: Installation, Quick Start, TUI Guide, Live Data Guide
- **Summary Documents**: Located in `docs/@docs/` directory
- **Main README**: [README.md](mdc:README.md) - Project overview and features

## Summary Documents Location

All summary and planning documents should be placed in `docs/@docs/`:

- `docs/@docs/UPGRADE_SUMMARY.md` - Major upgrades and improvements
- `docs/@docs/TUI_UX_IMPROVEMENT_PLAN.md` - TUI UX analysis and roadmap

## Documentation Files

- [docs/INSTALLATION.md](mdc:docs/INSTALLATION.md) - Installation instructions
- [docs/INSTALL_WINDOWS.md](mdc:docs/INSTALL_WINDOWS.md) - Windows-specific installation
- [docs/QUICKSTART.md](mdc:docs/QUICKSTART.md) - Quick start guide
- [docs/QUICK_START.md](mdc:docs/QUICK_START.md) - Alternative quick start
- [docs/TUI_GUIDE.md](mdc:docs/TUI_GUIDE.md) - TUI usage guide
- [docs/LIVE_DATA_GUIDE.md](mdc:docs/LIVE_DATA_GUIDE.md) - Live data streaming guide
- [docs/UV_USAGE.md](mdc:docs/UV_USAGE.md) - UV package manager usage

## Writing Documentation

- Use clear, concise language
- Include code examples where relevant
- Use proper Markdown formatting
- Link to related documentation
- Keep installation guides up-to-date with current dependencies

## Code Examples

- Always use `uv run` commands (not `python` directly)
- Show both Windows PowerShell and Unix shell examples when relevant
- Include expected output where helpful
- Use proper code fences with language tags
